eXcel schrieb:
> 
> carlhnel...@gmail.com schrieb:
> 
> > Hello,  I'm about to start my (first) ascension run and I'm having some
> > trouble deciding what to bring.  I am playing a lvl 30 Lawful Human
> > Archeologist with maxed out stats, full set of intrinsics, and a full
> > ascension kit, I think.  I have 700+ hp, AC -52, and 300 pw.

> > Even with my blessed bag of holding and STR 18/** I am still burdened
> > even without my assortment of potions (20+ full healing),
[...]

> > Do people really tend to stay unburdened on their ascension
> > run?

Yes. Being unburdened means you're a fair bit faster, and speed is of
the essence; it's not a coincidence it's called the ascension _run_.

Are you carrying around gold, by any chance? Gold is quite heavy and
generally not worth keeping.

> > So here are the basics of what I am bringing.  If anyone has any
> > suggestions, I'd sure appreciate the help.  Oh yeah, and I have a
> > saddled grey dragon.

I hope the dragon is hasted - zap a wand of speed monster at it until
you get the corresponding message. While in the saddle, you'll also
have maxed carrying capacity.

> I've only ascended once, so my end-game experience is somewhat limited,
> but here's what I would get rid of:
> 
> - 55 shuriken: I know I'll get flamed for this,

O.K.: Are you nucking futs?!? <g>

Seriously, though, missile weapons are very handy for taking out
priests (troublesome because they curse your stuff) and the riders at
moderate range. And shuriken are extremely lightweight - 55 weigh
something between 5 and 6 daggers, i think. Keeping, like you
suggested, only about 20 in the quiver and the rest in the bag might
still save a little bit of weight.

> - the small shield: when your AC is around -50, those 6 points from the
> shield won't make a difference, believe me. Get rid of that thing, now.

Damn right. I don't know how exactly the calculation goes, but even
under the assumption that AC can reduce damage to zero, going from -52
to -58 AC will only reduce the average damage sustained from a hit for
nominally 20 points from 4hp to 3,6hp; if AC can only reduce damage to
one point, the effect is even smaller - from 4,7hp to 4,5hp.
And for this fraction of a point of damage reduction, you'd be losing
around 10 points of damage from your secondary weapon, even after
accounting for the lower skill boni of twoweapon combat.

> - 5 wands of fire/cold: I'd take 2 of each at most. You've got wands of
> death and scrolls of charging.

I'd take only one wand of cold and a few of fire: at this point,
neither is useful as a mode of attack - wands of cold are only good
for freezing water (to retrieve items that went splash), wands of fire
for engraving Elbereth and as security against sliming.

> - 6 potions of booze: just zap a spell you didn't refresh after 20k
> turns, that will confuse you just fine.

Yes. Also remember that potions are relatively heavy. Not a good idea
carting around many unneeded ones.

> - 26 scrolls of teleport: hmm, just dig out straight paths and walk
> from stairwell to stairwell. Your idea is nice, but overkill in some
> way ;)

Won't work too well, either: teleport has a 1/3 chance of failing
while you're carrying the amulet, and in Gehennom, you'll traverse
many levels several times on the ascent, particularly when you're
lawful.

> - stethoscope: only keep it if you can cast healing spells reliably. No
> need to monitor your dragon's HP if you can't do anything once they get
> low.

Stethoscopes are so light that dropping one for weight reasons is just
ludicrous. The character is also wearing a helm of brilliance and
all-around spellfriendly armour, so i expect spells are likely on the
menu.

Even if you have no healing spells available, you can still search out
a nurse and feed it to your pet, lock it somewhere out of harm's way
or toss it a healing potion.

> Honestly, I'm somewhat surprised your BoH can't hold the stuff you
> listed without making you burdened.

The Orb of Detection makes a large difference for archaeologists.
Quite a chunk of extra weight.

